dev-concurrency-continuation.markdown:
A completion-handler API can interrupt the otherwise straightforward flow of async/await code. Continuations bridge that gap, but they also introduce an important responsibility: signal completion exactly once.

## From completion handlers to async/await

Continuations adapt older asynchronous patterns, such as completion handlers, for use with async/await. This makes the surrounding code easier to follow and lets error handling use the same structure as other asynchronous calls.

```swift
// Existing completion-handler API
URLSession.shared.dataTask(with: url) { data, response, error in
    if let data = data {
        // Handle a successful response
    } else if let error = error {
        // Handle a request error
    } else {
        // Handle other cases
    }
}
.resume()
```

## Checked and unsafe continuations

Both APIs suspend the current task until the continuation is resumed. Their main difference is the runtime checking they perform. Use the throwing variants when the operation can fail.

### withCheckedContinuation

The checked version is usually the best starting point. It detects incorrect use, including attempts to resume a continuation more than once.

### withUnsafeContinuation

The unsafe version omits those checks. This can reduce overhead, but incorrect use is harder to diagnose and can lead to undefined behavior.

> For throwing operations, use `withCheckedThrowingContinuation` or `withUnsafeThrowingContinuation`.

## Resuming a continuation

A continuation's `resume` methods signal completion and deliver a result or an error.

### resume(returning:)

Resumes a successful operation with its result.

### resume(throwing:)

Resumes a failed operation by throwing the supplied error.

> Every continuation must be resumed exactly once across all execution paths.

```swift
class ContinuationNetworkManager {
    func getDataAsync(url: URL) async throws -> Data {
    	// Bridge to async/await
        return try await withCheckedThrowingContinuation { continuation in
        	// Existing asynchronous implementation
            URLSession.shared.dataTask(with: url) { data, response, error in
                if let data = data {
                	// Return the result
                    continuation.resume(returning: data)
                } else if let error = error {
                	// Return the error
                    continuation.resume(throwing: error)
                } else {
	                // Return the error
                    continuation.resume(throwing: URLError(.badURL))
                }
            }
            .resume()
        }
    }
}

class ContinuationViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var image: UIImage? = nil
    let networkManager = ContinuationNetworkManager()

    func getImage() async {
        guard let url = URL(string: "https://picsum.photos/200") else { return }
        do {
        	// Call with async/await
            let data = try await networkManager.getDataAsync(url: url)
            if let image = UIImage(data: data) {
                await MainActor.run(body: {
                    self.image = image
                })
            }
        } catch {
            print(error)
        }
    }
}
```

dev-swift-strong-weak-unowned.markdown:
Two objects can keep each other alive long after they disappear from the screen. Understanding how ARC counts references helps explain how strong, weak, and unowned references change that relationship.

## Reference cycles

A cycle occurs when objects refer back to one another, such as A holding B while B holds A. If both references are strong, their reference counts cannot reach zero simply by removing outside references. The objects remain allocated, causing a memory leak.

```swift
class ClassA {
    var instanceB: ClassB?
    deinit {
        print("ClassA Deinitialized")
    }
}

class ClassB {
    var instanceA: ClassA?
    deinit {
        print("ClassB Deinitialized")
    }
}

var instanceA: ClassA? = ClassA()
var instanceB: ClassB? = ClassB()

// Reference each other
instanceA?.instanceB = instanceB
instanceB?.instanceA = instanceA

// Outside references are nil, but the strong reference cycle remains
instanceA = nil
instanceB = nil
```

## Strong references

Strong is the default reference type. A strong reference keeps an object alive by contributing to its reference count.

```swift
class Person {
    var pet: Pet?
}

class Pet {
    var owner: Person?
}

let snack = Person()
let cream = Pet()

snack.pet = cream
cream.owner = snack

// A strong reference cycle keeps snack and cream alive.
```

![](/image/development/2ff08a35e6097caf3f93.png)

## Weak references

A weak reference does not increase the object's reference count. It becomes `nil` when the object is deallocated, so a weak reference must be optional.

```swift
class Person {
    var pet: Pet?
}

class Pet {
    weak var owner: Person?
}

let snack = Person()
let cream = Pet()

snack.pet = cream
cream.owner = snack

// The weak owner reference becomes nil when its target is deallocated.
```

![](/image/development/5b913fd86ea973bbe0cc.png)

## Unowned references

An unowned reference does not increase the reference count either. Use it when the referenced object is guaranteed to remain alive for as long as the reference is accessed. Unlike a weak reference, it is not automatically set to `nil`. Accessing it after the object has been deallocated causes a runtime error.

```swift
class Person {
    var pet: Pet?
}

class Pet {
    unowned var owner: Person
    init(owner: Person) {
        self.owner = owner
    }
}

let snack = Person()
let cream = Pet(owner: snack)

snack.pet = cream

// Accessing cream.owner after snack is deallocated causes a runtime error.
```

![](/image/development/50bea7782e0d8acfec1d.png)

Further reading: [Swift: Automatic Reference Counting](https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/automaticreferencecounting/).

dev-concurrency-download-image-with-asyn.markdown:
Even downloading a single image can look very different depending on the asynchronous pattern. I compared escaping closures, Combine, and async/await to see how each affects control flow and error handling.

## Escaping closures

A completion handler is a traditional way to deliver the result of an asynchronous operation. Captures such as `[weak self]` help manage object lifetimes. This approach needs no additional reactive framework, but nested operations and error handling can become difficult to follow.

```swift
class DownloadImageAsyncImageLoader {
    let url = URL(string: "https://picsum.photos/200")!

    func handleResponse(data: Data?, response: URLResponse?) -> UIImage? {
        guard
            let data = data,
            let image = UIImage(data: data),
            let response = response as? HTTPURLResponse,
            response.statusCode >= 200, response.statusCode < 300
        else {
            return nil
        }
        return image
    }

    func downloadWithEscaping(completionHandler: @escaping (_ image: UIImage?, _ error: Error?) -> Void) {
        URLSession.shared.dataTask(with: url) { [weak self] data, response, error in
            let image = self?.handleResponse(data: data, response: response)
            completionHandler(image, error)
        }
        .resume()
    }
}

class DownloadImageAsyncViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var image: UIImage? = nil

    let loader = DownloadImageAsyncImageLoader()

    func fetchImage() {
        loader.downloadWithEscaping { [weak self] image, error in
            DispatchQueue.main.async {
                self?.image = image
            }
        }
    }
}

struct DownloadImageAsync: View {
    @StateObject private var viewModel = DownloadImageAsyncViewModel()

    var body: some View {
        ZStack {
            if let image = viewModel.image {
                Image(uiImage: image)
                    .resizable()
                    .scaledToFit()
                    .frame(width: 240, height: 240)
            }
        }
        .onAppear {
            viewModel.fetchImage()
        }
    }
}
```

## Combine

Combine models asynchronous work as streams of values. Subscriptions and cancellation provide a structured way to manage that work and its resources. It is available from iOS 13 and introduces concepts that take time to learn.

```swift
class DownloadImageAsyncImageLoader {
    let url = URL(string: "https://picsum.photos/200")!

    func handleResponse(data: Data?, response: URLResponse?) -> UIImage? {
        guard
            let data = data,
            let image = UIImage(data: data),
            let response = response as? HTTPURLResponse,
            response.statusCode >= 200, response.statusCode < 300
        else {
            return nil
        }
        return image
    }

    func downloadWithCombine() -> AnyPublisher<UIImage?, Error> {
        URLSession.shared.dataTaskPublisher(for: url)
            .map(handleResponse)
            .mapError { $0 }
            .eraseToAnyPublisher()
    }
}

class DownloadImageAsyncViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var image: UIImage? = nil
    let loader = DownloadImageAsyncImageLoader()
    var cancellables = Set<AnyCancellable>()

    func fetchImage() {
        loader.downloadWithCombine()
            .receive(on: DispatchQueue.main)
            .sink { _ in

            } receiveValue: { [weak self] image in
                self?.image = image
            }
            .store(in: &cancellables)
    }
}

struct DownloadImageAsync: View {
    @StateObject private var viewModel = DownloadImageAsyncViewModel()

    var body: some View {
        ZStack {
            if let image = viewModel.image {
                Image(uiImage: image)
                    .resizable()
                    .scaledToFit()
                    .frame(width: 240, height: 240)
            }
        }
        .onAppear {
            viewModel.fetchImage()
        }
    }
}
```

## Async/await

Async/await lets asynchronous code read much like sequential code, making control flow and error handling easier to follow. Tasks support asynchronous work and priorities, while `MainActor` provides isolation for UI-related state. Adoption depends on the deployment targets and APIs used by a project.

```swift
class DownloadImageAsyncImageLoader {
    let url = URL(string: "https://picsum.photos/200")!

    func handleResponse(data: Data?, response: URLResponse?) -> UIImage? {
        guard
            let data = data,
            let image = UIImage(data: data),
            let response = response as? HTTPURLResponse,
            response.statusCode >= 200, response.statusCode < 300
        else {
            return nil
        }
        return image
    }

    func downloadWithAsync() async throws -> UIImage? {
        do {
            let (data, response) = try await URLSession.shared.data(from: url)
            return handleResponse(data: data, response: response)
        } catch {
            throw error
        }
    }
}

class DownloadImageAsyncViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var image: UIImage? = nil
    let loader = DownloadImageAsyncImageLoader()
    var cancellables = Set<AnyCancellable>()

    func fetchImage() async {
        let image = try? await loader.downloadWithAsync()
        await MainActor.run {
            self.image = image
        }
    }
}

struct DownloadImageAsync: View {
    @StateObject private var viewModel = DownloadImageAsyncViewModel()

    var body: some View {
        ZStack {
            if let image = viewModel.image {
                Image(uiImage: image)
                    .resizable()
                    .scaledToFit()
                    .frame(width: 240, height: 240)
            }
        }
        .onAppear {
            Task {
               await viewModel.fetchImage()
            }
        }
    }
}
```

dev-swift-throw-try-do-catch.markdown:
Code that only describes success hides what happens when an operation fails. Swift makes that possibility visible at the call site with `throw` and `try`.

## Throwing errors

Use `throw` when an operation encounters a failure. First, define the possible errors, often with an enum that conforms to `Error`.

```swift
enum MyError: Error {
    case firstError
    case secondError
}
```

Mark the function with `throws` to show that it can fail. Inside it, use `throw` to report an error when the relevant condition occurs.

```swift
func someFunction() throws -> String {
	// Failure condition
	if isSuccess {
    	return "Success!"
    } else {
    	// Throw an error
    	throw MyError.firstError
    }
}
```

A call to a throwing function is marked with `try`.

## try and do-catch

A `do-catch` statement handles errors thrown by a `try` expression. The `catch` clauses specify how to respond to particular failures. A throwing caller can also propagate an error to its own caller.

```swift
// Call the throwing function with try
do {
	let result = try someFunction()
} catch MyError.firstError {
	// Handle the specific error
} catch {
	// Handle other errors
}
```

A `switch` inside `catch` is another way to handle different error cases.

```swift
do {
    let result = try someFunction()
} catch {
	// Handle each error case
    switch error {
    case MyError.firstError:
        print("first")
    case MyError.secondError:
        print("second")
    default:
        print("default")
    }
}
```

`try?` converts failure into `nil` and returns a successful result as an optional. It can be used without a `do-catch` block when the specific error is not needed.

```swift
// Return nil on failure
let result = try? someFunction()
```

Use `try!` only when you are certain the operation cannot throw in that context. If it does, execution stops with a runtime error.

```swift
// Trap if the call throws
let result = try! someFunction()
```

dev-swiftui-uiviewrepresentable.markdown:
Even in a SwiftUI interface, you may need an existing component or a view that UIKit provides. `UIViewRepresentable` creates a small bridge between the two frameworks.

## UIViewRepresentable

This protocol wraps a UIKit view for use in SwiftUI. Its core requirements are `makeUIView` and `updateUIView`.

## makeUIView

SwiftUI calls this method to create and initialize the UIKit view. Configure the initial appearance and behavior, then return the instance.

```swift
struct UIViewRepresentableStudy: View {
    var body: some View {
        BasicUIViewRepresentable()
    }
}

struct BasicUIViewRepresentable: UIViewRepresentable {

    // Create and configure the UIView
    func makeUIView(context: Context) -> some UIView {
        let view = UIView()
        view.backgroundColor = .blue
        return view
    }

    func updateUIView(_ uiView: UIViewType, context: Context) {

    }
}
```

![](/image/development/d39496185fab60a1432d.png)

## updateUIView

SwiftUI calls this method when the represented view needs to reflect updated state. A binding can carry data from SwiftUI into the UIKit view.

```swift
struct UIViewRepresentableStudy: View {

    @State private var text: String = ""

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text(text)
            TextField("SwiftUI Type here..", text: $text)
                .frame(height: 56)
                .background(Color.gray)
            UITextFieldViewRepresentable(text: $text)
                .frame(height: 56)
                .background(Color.gray)
        }
    }
}

struct UITextFieldViewRepresentable: UIViewRepresentable {

    @Binding var text: String

    // Specify UITextField as the return type
    func makeUIView(context: Context) -> UITextField {
        let textfield = UITextField(frame: .zero)
        let placeholder = NSAttributedString(string: "UIKit Type here...", attributes: [.foregroundColor : UIColor.white])
        textfield.attributedPlaceholder = placeholder
        return textfield
    }

    func updateUIView(_ uiView: UITextField, context: Context) {
    	// Update UIKit from SwiftUI
        uiView.text = text
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/a9a8b4f3b158300eb4e4.gif)

## Coordinators

A coordinator handles communication in the other direction, from UIKit back to SwiftUI. Define a `Coordinator` class inside the representable and return an instance from `makeCoordinator`. In this example, the coordinator becomes the text field's delegate and updates the binding when the text changes.

```swift
struct UIViewRepresentableStudy: View {

    @State private var text: String = ""

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text(text)
            TextField("SwiftUI Type here..", text: $text)
                .frame(height: 56)
                .background(Color.gray)
            UITextFieldViewRepresentable(text: $text)
                .frame(height: 56)
                .background(Color.gray)
        }
    }
}

struct UITextFieldViewRepresentable: UIViewRepresentable {

    @Binding var text: String

    func makeUIView(context: Context) -> UITextField {
        let textfield = UITextField(frame: .zero)
        let placeholder = NSAttributedString(string: "UIKit Type here...", attributes: [.foregroundColor : UIColor.white])
        textfield.attributedPlaceholder = placeholder
        // Set the coordinator as the delegate
        textfield.delegate = context.coordinator
        return textfield
    }

    func updateUIView(_ uiView: UITextField, context: Context) {
        uiView.text = text
    }

	// Implement makeCoordinator
    func makeCoordinator() -> Coordinator {
        return Coordinator(text: $text)
    }

	// Define the coordinator
    class Coordinator: NSObject, UITextFieldDelegate {

        @Binding var text: String

        init(text: Binding<String>) {
            self._text = text
        }

        func textFieldDidChangeSelection(_ textField: UITextField) {
        	// Update SwiftUI from UIKit
            text = textField.text ?? ""
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/2072d89ae85f09c2cbc5.gif)

dev-swiftui-matchedgeometryeffect.markdown:
When one view replaces another, a basic animation may not make the two states feel connected. `matchedGeometryEffect` lets SwiftUI treat them as representations of the same visual element.

## Matched geometry

A typical animation interpolates changes within a view. Matched geometry coordinates changes between views. Give the related views the same `id` within a shared `@Namespace` so SwiftUI can connect their geometry.

```swift
struct MatchedGeometryEffectStudy: View {

    @State private var isClicked: Bool = false
    @Namespace private var namespace

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            if !isClicked {
                RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 12)
                	// Match this view for the animation
                    .matchedGeometryEffect(id: "rectangle", in: namespace)
                    .frame(width: 100, height: 100)
            }
            Spacer()
            if isClicked {
                RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 12)
					// Match this view for the animation
                    .matchedGeometryEffect(id: "rectangle", in: namespace)
                    .frame(width: 200, height: 200)
            }
        }
        .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, maxHeight: .infinity)
        .background(.red)
        .onTapGesture {
            withAnimation(.easeInOut) {
                isClicked.toggle()
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/207a962ff2cb1e57cc60.gif)

The same technique can animate the selection indicator in a segmented control.

```swift
struct MatchedGeometryEffectStudy2: View {

    let categories: [String] = ["Home", "Popular", "Saved"]
    @State private var selected: String = "Home"
    @Namespace private var namespace2

    var body: some View {
        HStack {
            ForEach(categories, id: \.self) { category in
                ZStack {
                    if selected == category {
                        RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 12)
                            .fill(Color.blue).opacity(0.3)
							// Match this view for the animation
                            .matchedGeometryEffect(id: "category", in: namespace2)
                    }
                    Text(category)
                }
                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
                .frame(height: 56)
                .onTapGesture {
                    withAnimation(.spring()) {
                        selected = category
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/004aa6c3a03d79b38bac.gif)

dev-swift-generic.markdown:
When several functions do the same work for different types, duplication quickly becomes more noticeable than the implementation itself. Generics let us write the shared behavior once and supply the concrete type later.

## Generic functions

A type parameter such as `<T>` lets a function work with different types without giving up type safety.

```swift
var a = 2
var b = 3
var apple = "apple"
var banana = "banana"

func swapElements<T>(_ first: inout T, _ second: inout T) {
    let temp = first
    first = second
    second = temp
}

swapElements(&a, &b) // Int values
swapElements(&apple, &banana) // String values
```

Separate type parameters with commas when a function needs more than one.

```swift
func someFunction<One, Two>(_ first: One, _ second: Two) { ... }
```

## Generic types

Structures, classes, and enumerations can have type parameters too.

```swift
struct Stack<Element> {
    var items = [Element]()

    mutating func push(_ item: Element) {
        items.append(item)
    }
    mutating func pop() -> Element {
        return items.removeLast()
    }
}
```

When creating an instance, provide the concrete type, either explicitly or through type inference where possible.

```swift
let stack1: Stack<Int> = .init() // An instance with Int elements
let stack2 = Stack<String>.init() // An instance with String elements
```

## Extending generic types

An extension can use the generic type's existing type parameters. It does not redeclare them.

```swift
extension Array {
    mutating func pop() -> Element {
        return self.removeLast()
    }
}
```

## Type constraints

Constraints can require a type parameter to inherit from a particular class or conform to a protocol.

```swift
func someFunction<T: SomeClass, U: SomeProtocol>(someT: T, someU: U) { ... }
```

dev-swiftui-safeareainset.markdown:
A fixed button at the bottom of a screen can cover the final items in the content. Rather than simply overlaying another view, `safeAreaInset` adjusts the safe area available to the surrounding content.

## safeAreaInset

Use this modifier to place a view along an edge and reserve space for it.

```swift
struct SafeAreaInsetStudy: View {
    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            List(0..<10) { _ in
                Rectangle()
                    .frame(height: 200)
            }
            .navigationTitle("Safe Area")
            // Add a view at the bottom safe-area edge
            .safeAreaInset(edge: .bottom, alignment: .center, spacing: nil) {
                Button("test") {
                    // test button code
                }
                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
                .background(Color.green)
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/5011f9b667fd3c569a8c.png)

When adding a top inset inside a navigation container, the navigation-title area affects where the added view and its background appear.

```swift
struct SafeAreaInsetStudy: View {
    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            List(0..<10) { _ in
                Rectangle()
                    .frame(height: 200)
            }
            .navigationTitle("Safe Area")
            // Add a view at the top safe-area edge
            .safeAreaInset(edge: .top, alignment: .center, spacing: nil) {
                Button("test") {

                }
                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
                .background(Color.green)
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/c9ab2f046687432a0d62.png)

Changing which safe-area edges the inserted view's background ignores changes how that background extends around the navigation area, as shown below.

```swift
struct SafeAreaInsetStudy: View {
    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            List(0..<10) { _ in
                Rectangle()
                    .frame(height: 200)
            }
            .navigationTitle("Safe Area")
            .safeAreaInset(edge: .top, alignment: .center, spacing: nil) {
                Button("test") {
                    // test button code
                }
                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
                // Let the inserted background ignore the bottom safe area
                .background(Color.green.edgesIgnoringSafeArea(.bottom))
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/0c7110b73d8676fc83ab.png)

dev-swiftui-resizable-sheet.markdown:
A sheet does not always need to fill the screen. Detents let it start small for a short interaction and expand when more space is needed.

## presentationDetents

- Use `presentationDetents` to specify a set of available sheet heights.
- Detents can use a fraction of the available height, a fixed height, or a standard size such as `.medium` or `.large`.
- A sheet with multiple sizes can show a drag indicator. Control its visibility with `presentationDragIndicator`.
- Use `interactiveDismissDisabled` when the sheet should not be dismissed through its usual interactive gesture.

```swift
struct SheetStudy: View {

    @State var showSheet: Bool = false

    var body: some View {

        Button("Click Me") {
            showSheet.toggle()
        }
        .sheet(isPresented: $showSheet) {
            ModalView()
                .presentationDetents([
                	.fraction(0.1), // Custom fraction
                    .height(300), // Custom height
                    .medium, // Medium detent
                    .large // Large detent
                    ])
                // Hide the drag indicator
                .presentationDragIndicator(.hidden)
                // Disable interactive dismissal
                .interactiveDismissDisabled()
        }
        .onAppear {
            showSheet = true
        }
    }
}
```

## Selection

The `selection` binding tracks the current detent and lets your code change it. Set it to a value included in the available detents.

```swift
struct SheetStudy: View {

    @State var showSheet: Bool = false
    @State var detents: PresentationDetent = .medium

    var body: some View {

        Button("Click Me") {
            showSheet.toggle()
            detents = .large
        }
        .sheet(isPresented: $showSheet) {
            ModalView(detents: $detents)
            	// Bind the selected detent
                .presentationDetents([.medium, .large], selection: $detents)
        }
        .onAppear {
            showSheet = true
        }
    }

}

struct ModalView: View {

    @Binding var detents: PresentationDetent

    var body: some View {
        ZStack {
            Color(.green).ignoresSafeArea()
            VStack {
                Button("medium") {
                    detents = .medium // Select the medium detent
                }
                Button("large") {
                    detents = .large // Select the large detent
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/e15d86cff18a48477bf6.png)

dev-swiftui-publishers-subscribers.markdown:
Subscribing to a stream of changes creates a different relationship than calling a function whenever a value changes. Publishers and subscribers describe how values are produced, delivered, and completed.

## Publishers and subscribers

A `Publisher` emits values or events from a data source. A `Subscriber` receives those values and the stream's completion event.

```swift
class SubscriberViewModel: ObservableObject {

    @Published var count: Int = 0
    // Store an AnyCancellable
    var timer: AnyCancellable?

    init() {
        setUpTimer()
    }

    func setUpTimer() {
    	// Publisher that emits at regular intervals
        timer = Timer
            .publish(every: 1, on: .main, in: .common)
            .autoconnect()
            // Subscriber that receives and handles events
            .sink { [weak self] _ in
                self?.count += 1
                if let count = self?.count, count >= 10 {
                	// Cancel the subscription and stop its events
                    self?.timer?.cancel()
                }
            }
    }
}
```

`Cancellable` represents work that can be canceled. Calling `cancel` ends the subscription's work and releases associated resources. `AnyCancellable` is a type-erased cancellation token that cancels when deinitialized. Store tokens in a `Set` with `store(in:)` to keep subscriptions alive and manage their lifetimes, much like a dispose bag in RxSwift.

```swift
class SubscriberViewModel: ObservableObject {

    @Published var count: Int = 0
    // Store cancellation tokens
    var cancellables = Set<AnyCancellable>()

    init() {
        setUpTimer()
    }

    func setUpTimer() {
        Timer
            .publish(every: 1, on: .main, in: .common)
            .autoconnect()
            .sink { [weak self] _ in
            	// Unwrap with guard let
                guard let self = self else { return }
                self.count += 1
                if self.count >= 10 {
					// Cancel the subscription and stop its events
                    for item in self.cancellables {
                        item.cancel()
                    }
                }
            }
            // Store the cancellation token returned by sink
            .store(in: &cancellables)
    }
}
```

- `map` transforms each incoming value.
- `assign` writes emitted values to a property.
- A `[weak self]` capture in `sink` can prevent a strong reference cycle.
- `debounce` waits for a period without new input before delivering the latest value.

```swift
class SubscriberViewModel: ObservableObject {

    var cancellables = Set<AnyCancellable>()

    @Published var textFieldText: String = ""
    @Published var textIsValid: Bool = false

    init() {
        addTextFieldSubscriber()
    }

    func addTextFieldSubscriber() {
        $textFieldText
			// Debounce the latest value
	        .debounce(for: .seconds(0.5), scheduler: DispatchQueue.main)
			// Transform the emitted value
            .map { text -> Bool in
                if text.count > 3 {
                    return true
                }
                return false
            }
            // Assign published values to a property
            // .assign(to: \.textIsValid, on: self)
            // Capture self weakly to avoid a reference cycle
            .sink(receiveValue: { [weak self] isValid in
                self?.textIsValid = isValid
            })
            .store(in: &cancellables)
    }
}
```

`combineLatest` combines publishers using the latest value from each, producing a new output when an upstream value changes after all inputs have emitted at least once.

```swift
class SubscriberViewModel: ObservableObject {

    @Published var count: Int = 0
    var cancellables = Set<AnyCancellable>()

    @Published var textFieldText: String = ""
    @Published var textIsValid: Bool = false

    @Published var showButton: Bool = false

    init() {
        setUpTimer()
        addTextFieldSubscriber()
        addButtonSubscriber()
    }

    func setUpTimer() {
        Timer
            .publish(every: 1, on: .main, in: .common)
            .autoconnect()
            .sink { [weak self] _ in
                guard let self = self else { return }
                self.count += 1
            }
            .store(in: &cancellables)
    }

    func addTextFieldSubscriber() {
        $textFieldText
            .debounce(for: .seconds(0.5), scheduler: DispatchQueue.main)
            .map { text -> Bool in
                if text.count > 3 {
                    return true
                }
                return false
            }
        //            .assign(to: \.textIsValid, on: self)
            .sink(receiveValue: { [weak self] isValid in
                self?.textIsValid = isValid
            })
            .store(in: &cancellables)
    }

    func addButtonSubscriber() {
        $textIsValid
        	// Combine two publishers
            .combineLatest($count)
            .sink { [weak self] isValid, count in
                guard let self = self else { return }
                if isValid && count >= 10 {
                    self.showButton = true
                } else {
                    self.showButton = false
                }
            }
            .store(in: &cancellables)
    }
}
```

