dev-swift-escaping.markdown:
Whether a closure runs before or after its enclosing function returns affects its lifetime and captures. `@escaping` makes that longer lifetime explicit.

## Nonescaping closures

By default, a closure parameter cannot outlive the function call. The function must use it without storing it for later execution.

```swift
class EscapingViewModel: ObservableObject {

    @Published var text: String = "Hello"

    func getData() {
        downloadData { data in
            text = data
        }
    }

    func downloadData(completion: (_ data: String) -> Void) {
    	// Run the closure before returning
        completion("New Data!")
    }
}
```

## Escaping closures

An `@escaping` closure can be stored or called after the function returns. This is common in asynchronous operations that deliver a result later.

```swift
class EscapingViewModel: ObservableObject {

    @Published var text: String = "Hello"

    func getData() {
    	// Capture weakly for asynchronous work
        downloadData { [weak self] data in
            self?.text = data
        }
    }

    func downloadData(completion: @escaping (_ data: String) -> Void) {
	    // Run the closure two seconds after the function returns
        DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + 2.0) {
            completion("New Data!")
        }
    }
}
```

## Models and type aliases

A data model makes related values easier to access and pass together.

```swift
class EscapingViewModel: ObservableObject {

    @Published var text: String = "Hello"

    func getData() {
        downloadData { [weak self] DownloadResult in
        	// Access the data
            self?.text = DownloadResult.data
        }
    }

    func downloadData(completion: @escaping (DownloadResult) -> Void) {
        DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + 2.0) {
        	let result = DownloadResult(data: "New Data")
            completion(result)
        }
    }
}

// Create the data model
struct DownloadResult {
    let data: String
}
```

A `typealias` can give a closure type a concise, descriptive name.

```swift
class EscapingViewModel: ObservableObject {

    @Published var text: String = "Hello"

    func getData() {
        downloadData { [weak self] DownloadResult in
            self?.text = DownloadResult.data
        }
    }

    // Closure parameter using the type alias
    func downloadData(completion: @escaping DownloadCompletion) {
        DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + 2.0) {
        	let result = DownloadResult(data: "New Data")
            completion(result)
        }
    }
}

struct DownloadResult {
    let data: String
}

// Define a type alias
typealias DownloadCompletion = (DownloadResult) -> Void
```

dev-swiftui-state.markdown:
SwiftUI views are value types, yet the numbers and selections they display keep changing. `@State` connects persistent state storage with the updates a view needs.

## @State in a structure

A view's `body` is computed from its current data. `@State` gives SwiftUI a place to manage a value outside the transient view value and update dependent views when it changes. Declare state as `private` to keep ownership within the view.

```swift
struct StateStudy: View {

    // Observe value changes
    @State private var count: Int = 0

    var body: some View {
        VStack(spacing: 24) {
            Text("Current Count is \(count)")
            Button("Click to Count") {
                count += 1
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/4dd27e32da8c2e255258.gif)

dev-swiftui-environmentobject.markdown:
Passing state through several levels can force intermediate views to carry data they never use. An environment object lets a descendant access a shared object without passing it through every initializer.

## Passing data between views

`@StateObject` and `@ObservedObject` allow views to share an `ObservableObject`. Explicitly passing that reference works, but intermediate views may need properties solely to forward it to their children.

```swift
// Shared data
class EnvironmentViewModel: ObservableObject {

    @Published var dataArray: [String] = []

    init() {
        getData()
    }

    func getData() {
        self.dataArray.append(contentsOf: ["iPhone", "iPad", "iMac"])
    }
}

// First view
struct FirstView: View {

    // Create data
    @StateObject var viewModel: EnvironmentViewModel = EnvironmentViewModel()

    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            List {
                ForEach(viewModel.dataArray, id: \.self) { item in
                    NavigationLink {
                        SecondView(viewModel: viewModel, selectedItem: item)
                    } label: {
                        Text(item)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

// Second view
struct SecondView: View {

    // Forward data through an intermediate view
    @ObservedObject var viewModel: EnvironmentViewModel
    let selectedItem: String

    var body: some View {
        NavigationLink {
            ThirdView(viewModel: viewModel)
        } label: {
            Text(selectedItem)
        }
    }
}

// Third view
struct ThirdView: View {

    // Share the data
    @ObservedObject var viewModel: EnvironmentViewModel

    var body: some View {
        ForEach(viewModel.dataArray, id: \.self) { item in
            Text(item)
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/324efad6719cf94149ad.png)

## @EnvironmentObject

A parent can own an object with `@StateObject` and inject it using `.environmentObject()`. Descendants read the shared instance with `@EnvironmentObject`, without requiring every intermediate view to pass it along.

```swift
struct FirstView: View {

    // Create data
    @StateObject var viewModel: EnvironmentViewModel = EnvironmentViewModel()

    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            List {
                ForEach(viewModel.dataArray, id: \.self) { item in
                    NavigationLink {
                        SecondView(selectedItem: item)
                    } label: {
                        Text(item)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        // Share the object with descendants
        .environmentObject(viewModel)
    }
}

struct SecondView: View {

    // No intermediate forwarding needed
    let selectedItem: String

    var body: some View {
        NavigationLink {
            ThirdView()
        } label: {
            Text(selectedItem)
        }
    }
}

struct ThirdView: View {

    // Share the data
    @EnvironmentObject var viewModel: EnvironmentViewModel

    var body: some View {
        ForEach(viewModel.dataArray, id: \.self) { item in
            Text(item)
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/40798b459b009ecbdb4c.png)

dev-swiftui-observedobject-stateobject.markdown:
As a screen grows, keeping its data, logic, and interface in one structure becomes difficult. It matters not only how a view observes an object, but also who creates and owns that object.

## Separating responsibilities

Separating code improves collaboration, maintenance, and extensibility. One useful division is the data model, the interface, and the logic that connects them.

```swift
// Model
struct FruitModel: Identifiable {
    let id: String = UUID().uuidString
    let name: String
    let count: Int
}

struct ViewModelStudy: View {

    @State var fruitArray: [FruitModel] = [
        FruitModel(name: "Apple", count: 5)
    ]

    // View
    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            List {
                ForEach(fruitArray) { fruit in
                    HStack {
                        Text("\(fruit.count)")
                            .foregroundColor(.red)
                        Text(fruit.name)
                    }
                }
                .onDelete { indexSet in
                    deleteFruit(index: indexSet)
                }
            }
            .onAppear {
                getFruits()
            }
        }
    }

    // View model
    func getFruits() {
        let fruit1 = FruitModel(name: "Banana", count: 7)
        let fruit2 = FruitModel(name: "Carrot", count: 3)
        fruitArray.append(fruit1)
        fruitArray.append(fruit2)
    }
    func deleteFruit(index: IndexSet) {
        fruitArray.remove(atOffsets: index)
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/e37cd217dc14da991c77.png)

> **MVVM architecture**
> Model–View–ViewModel is one way to organize these responsibilities in a SwiftUI application.

![](/image/development/b0f06463f57f33ed75d8.png)

## Model: the data

Represent application data separately using structures, arrays, and other suitable types.

```swift
struct FruitModel: Identifiable {
    let id: String = UUID().uuidString
    let name: String
    let count: Int
}
```

## View model: logic and behavior

A view model can be a class conforming to `ObservableObject`. It holds the data and behavior the view needs and publishes changes. Properties that should notify observers can use `@Published`.

> **@Published**
> Changes to a published property notify observers so SwiftUI can update views that depend on the object.

```swift
class FruitViewModel: ObservableObject {

    // Observe the data model
    @Published var fruitArray: [FruitModel] = [
        FruitModel(name: "Apple", count: 5)
    ]

    init() {
        getFruits()
    }

    func getFruits() {
        let fruit1 = FruitModel(name: "Banana", count: 7)
        let fruit2 = FruitModel(name: "Carrot", count: 3)
        fruitArray.append(fruit1)
        fruitArray.append(fruit2)
    }
    func deleteFruit(index: IndexSet) {
        fruitArray.remove(atOffsets: index)
    }
}
```

## View: the interface

The view describes what the user sees and interacts with. It can observe a view model using `@ObservedObject`, or own one using `@StateObject`.

```swift
struct ViewModelStudy: View {

    // Observe the view model
    @ObservedObject var fruitViewModel: FruitViewModel = FruitViewModel()

    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            List {
                ForEach(fruitViewModel.fruitArray) { fruit in
                    HStack {
                        Text("\(fruit.count)")
                            .foregroundColor(.red)
                        Text(fruit.name)
                    }
                }
                .onDelete(perform: fruitViewModel.deleteFruit)
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## @ObservedObject versus @StateObject

Both wrappers let SwiftUI observe an object. The important difference is ownership and lifetime, not whether the entire view or only part of it is redrawn.

| Aspect | @ObservedObject | @StateObject |
|---|---|---|
| Updates | Observes published changes and invalidates dependent view content. | Observes published changes and invalidates dependent view content. |
| Lifetime | Observes an instance supplied and owned elsewhere. It does not provide persistent ownership for a newly created instance across view updates. | SwiftUI creates and retains the instance for the lifetime of the owning view's identity. |
| Typical use | A child view receiving an existing object. | A view responsible for creating and owning the object. |

```swift
struct ViewModelStudy: View {

    // The parent owns the model with @StateObject
    @StateObject var fruitViewModel: FruitViewModel = FruitViewModel()

    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            List {
                ForEach(fruitViewModel.fruitArray) { fruit in
                    HStack {
                        Text("\(fruit.count)")
                            .foregroundColor(.red)
                        Text(fruit.name)
                    }
                }
                .onDelete(perform: fruitViewModel.deleteFruit)
            }
            .toolbar {
                ToolbarItem(placement: .navigationBarTrailing) {
                    NavigationLink {
                    	// Pass the same view model to the child
                        RandomScreen(fruitViewModel: fruitViewModel)
                    } label: {
                        Image(systemName: "arrow.right")
                    }

                }
            }
        }
    }
}

struct RandomScreen: View {

    // The child observes the supplied model
    @ObservedObject var fruitViewModel: FruitViewModel
    @Environment(\.presentationMode) var presentationMode

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            ForEach(fruitViewModel.fruitArray) { fruit in
                Text(fruit.name)
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/bbe932a856370328ed2e.png)

Further reading: [Apple: StateObject](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/stateobject).

dev-swiftui-custom-model.markdown:
An array may be enough when a screen displays only a few values. As properties and relationships grow, a dedicated model makes the code's intent much clearer.

## Arrays

An `Array` stores values of one type in order. Individual elements can be accessed by index.

```swift
struct CustomModelStudy: View {

    // Array-based data model
    @State var users: [String] = [
        "Snack", "Koby", "Hannah", "Kayle", "Goggins"
    ]

    var body: some View {
        List {
        	// Access an element by index
            ForEach(users, id: \.self) { name in
                HStack(spacing: 16) {
                    Circle()
                        .frame(width: 32, height: 32)
                    Text(name)
                }
                .padding(.vertical, 4)
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/12eae14111238344a2dd.png)

## Structures

A `struct` groups related properties, even when they have different types. Conforming to `Identifiable` gives each instance an identity that SwiftUI can track. A `UUID` is one option for that identifier.

> **UUID**
> A universally unique identifier is a 128-bit value, commonly displayed as 32 hexadecimal digits separated by hyphens, such as `E621E1F8-C36C-495A-93FC-0C247A3E6E5F`.

```swift
struct UserModel: Identifiable {
	// Create a UUID string
    let id: String = UUID().uuidString
    let displayName: String
    let userName: String
    let followerCount: Int
    let isVerified: Bool
}

struct CustomModelStudy: View {

    @State var users: [UserModel] = [
   		// Create an instance for each item
        UserModel(displayName: "Snack", userName: "snack123", followerCount: 100, isVerified: false),
        UserModel(displayName: "Koby", userName: "koby123", followerCount: 200, isVerified: true),
        UserModel(displayName: "Hannah", userName: "hannah123", followerCount: 300, isVerified: false)
    ]

    var body: some View {
        List {
            ForEach(users) { user in
            	// Group properties of different types
                HStack(spacing: 16) {
                    Circle()
                        .frame(width: 40, height: 40)
                    VStack(alignment: .leading) {
                        Text(user.displayName)
                        Text("@\(user.userName)")
                            .foregroundColor(.gray)
                            .font(.caption)
                    }
                    if user.isVerified {
                        Image(systemName: "checkmark.seal.fill")
                            .foregroundColor(.blue)
                    }
                    Spacer()
                    VStack {
                        Text("\(user.followerCount)")
                            .font(.headline)
                        Text("Followers")
                            .foregroundColor(.gray)
                            .font(.caption)
                    }
                }
                .padding(.vertical, 4)
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/5a9f8a0cde7b1c61abbb.png)

dev-swiftui-geometryreader.markdown:
A layout based only on the device's screen size may not adapt to split-screen use or a changing parent view. `GeometryReader` provides information about the space available to the view itself.

## UIScreen

Screen dimensions can be used to calculate widths and positions, but they do not necessarily describe the current container. This makes screen-based calculations fragile when the window or layout changes.

```swift
HStack(spacing: 0) {
    Rectangle()
        .fill(.red)
        // Two-thirds of the screen width
        .frame(width: UIScreen.main.bounds.width * 0.666)
    Rectangle()
        .fill(.blue)
}
.ignoresSafeArea()
```

![](/image/development/4275b1c115d12eb297ad.png)

## GeometryReader

The `GeometryProxy` supplied to the closure exposes size and coordinate information. Use it to calculate a child's layout within the available space. Because geometry-driven changes can trigger additional layout work, use it where that information is actually needed.

```swift
GeometryReader { geometry in
    HStack(spacing: 0) {
        Rectangle()
            .fill(.red)
            // Two-thirds of the parent's width
            .frame(width: geometry.size.width * 0.666)
        Rectangle()
            .fill(.blue)
    }
    .ignoresSafeArea()
}
```

![](/image/development/8092bf678b8fcf4d475f.png)

## Reading a frame

`frame(in:)` returns the view's frame in a specified coordinate space.

```swift
enum CoordinateSpace {
  case global // Relative to the global coordinate space
  case local // Relative to the GeometryReader
  case named(AnyHashable) // Relative to the named coordinate space
}    
```

![](/image/development/592a4df3015032cc8b3d.png)

Geometry information can also drive visual effects as a view changes position.

```swift
struct GeometryReaderStudy: View {
    var body: some View {
        ScrollView(.horizontal, showsIndicators: false) {
            HStack {
                ForEach(0..<20) { index in
                    GeometryReader { geometry in
                        RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 20)
                            .rotation3DEffect(
                            	// Rotate the card based on its position
                                Angle(degrees: getPercentage(geo: geometry)) * 20,
                                axis: (x: 0.0, y: 1.0, z: 0.0))
                    }
                    .frame(width: 300, height: 250)
                    .padding()
                }
            }
        }
    }
    func getPercentage(geo: GeometryProxy) -> Double {
    	// The screen midpoint
        let maxDistance = UIScreen.main.bounds.width / 2
        // The card's current midpoint in global coordinates
        let currentX = geo.frame(in: .global).midX
        // Calculate the relative position
        return Double(1 - (currentX / maxDistance))
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/0b80b14215485a2d3d44.gif)

dev-swiftui-scrollviewreader.markdown:
Jumping to an item in a long list is easier when we identify the item rather than calculate a scroll distance. `ScrollViewReader` provides a proxy for scrolling to views by ID.

## Controls inside the scroll view

Assign an `id` to the target view and call `scrollTo` through the proxy. The `anchor` determines how the target aligns with the visible area.

```swift
ScrollView {
    ScrollViewReader { proxy in
        Button("Click here to go to #49") {
            withAnimation(.spring()) {
            	// Scroll to the view with this ID
                proxy.scrollTo(30, anchor: .center)
            }
        }
        ForEach(0..<50) { index in
            Text("This is item #\(index)")
                .font(.headline)
                .frame(height: 200)
                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
                .background(Color.white)
                .cornerRadius(16)
                .shadow(radius: 10)
                .padding()
                .id(index) // Create an ID
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/71822ec2e2a9a6907594.png)

## Controls outside the scroll view

A `TextField` and `onChange` can drive scrolling from a control outside the scrolling content.

```swift
struct ScrollViewReaderStudy: View {

    @State var scrollToIndex: Int = 0
    @State var textFieldText: String = ""

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
        	// Enter the target position as a string
            TextField("scroll number", text: $textFieldText)
                .frame(height: 56)
                .border(.gray)
                .padding(.horizontal)
                .keyboardType(.numberPad)
            Button("Scroll Now") {
            	// Convert the input to an Int position
                if let index = Int(textFieldText) {
                    scrollToIndex = index
                }
            }
            ScrollView {
                ScrollViewReader { proxy in
                    ForEach(0..<50) { index in
                        Text("This is item #\(index)")
                            .font(.headline)
                            .frame(height: 200)
                            .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
                            .background(Color.white)
                            .cornerRadius(16)
                            .shadow(radius: 10)
                            .padding()
                            .id(index)
                    }
                    // Scroll when the integer target changes
                    .onChange(of: scrollToIndex) { newValue in
                        withAnimation {
                            proxy.scrollTo(newValue, anchor: .center)
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/1b13f43c7028ec6cc226.png)

dev-swiftui-navigationstack.markdown:
Treating navigation as a collection of links makes a deep route difficult to control in code. `NavigationStack` can represent the route as data that the application can read and change.

## NavigationLink

A navigation link pushes a destination onto the stack and lets the user return to the previous screen.

```swift
NavigationStack {
    List {
    	// Navigate to each destination
        NavigationLink("Red", destination: Text("Red"))
        NavigationLink("Green", destination: Text("Green"))
        NavigationLink("Blue", destination: Text("Blue"))
    }
    .navigationTitle("Colors") // Navigation title
}
```

## navigationDestination

This modifier associates a value type with a destination view. A value-based link describes the data to navigate to, while the destination builder defines the corresponding screen.

```swift
NavigationStack {
    List {
        NavigationLink("Red", value: Color.red)
        NavigationLink("Green", value: Color.green)
        NavigationLink("Blue", value: Color.blue)
    }
    // Define destinations for values of this type
    .navigationDestination(for: Color.self, destination: { color in
        ColorDetail(color: color)
    })
    .navigationTitle("Colors")
}
```

The value passed to `NavigationLink` must match a registered destination type for navigation to work.

```swift
struct ColorDetail: View {

    var color: Color

    var body: some View {
            Text("\(self.color.description)")
    }
}
```

Register multiple destination types when a stack needs to handle different kinds of values.

```swift
NavigationStack {
    List {
        NavigationLink("Red", value: Color.red)
        NavigationLink("Green", value: Color.green)
        NavigationLink("Blue", value: "blue")
    }
    .navigationDestination(for: Color.self, destination: { color in
        ColorDetailForColor(color: color) // Color type
    })
    .navigationDestination(for: String.self, destination: { color in
        ColorDetailForString(color: color) // String values
    })
    .navigationTitle("Colors")
}
```

## Navigation state

Store a `NavigationPath` in state to track the route. Removing the last value returns to the previous screen; clearing the path returns to the root.

```swift
struct NavigationStackStudy: View {

    // Track the navigation path
    @State var stack: NavigationPath = NavigationPath()

    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack(path: $stack) {
            List {
                NavigationLink("Red", value: Color.red)
                NavigationLink("Green", value: Color.green)
                NavigationLink("Blue", value: Color.blue)
            }
            .navigationDestination(for: Color.self, destination: { color in
                ColorDetail(stack: $stack, color: color)
				NavigationLink("Yellow", value: Color.yellow) // A stack of repeated content

            })
            .navigationTitle("Colors")
        }
    }
}

struct ColorDetail: View {

    @Binding var stack: NavigationPath
    var color: Color

    var body: some View {
        Text("\(self.color.description)")
        Button("Back") {
            stack.removeLast() // Return to the previous screen
        }
        Button("First") {
        	stack = .init() // Return to the root screen
        }
    }
}
```

Setting the path programmatically also supports navigating directly to a particular route, including deep links.

```swift
struct NavigationStackStudy: View {

    // Track the navigation path
    @State var stack: [Color] = []

    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack(path: $stack) {
            List {
                NavigationLink("Red", value: Color.red)
                NavigationLink("Green", value: Color.green)
                NavigationLink("Blue", value: Color.blue)
                Button("Go to Yellow") {
                	// Set the path directly
                    stack = [.red, .green, .blue, .yellow]
                }
            }
            .navigationDestination(for: Color.self, destination: { color in
                ColorDetail(color: color)
            })
            .navigationTitle("Colors")
        }
    }
}

struct ColorDetail: View {

    var color: Color

    var body: some View {
        Text("\(self.color.description)")
    }
}
```

dev-swiftui-appstorage.markdown:
Small preferences, such as a theme choice or whether onboarding is complete, should survive an app restart. `@AppStorage` connects key–value storage in `UserDefaults` to SwiftUI updates with a small declaration.

## UserDefaults

`UserDefaults` stores simple settings and preferences as key–value pairs. Each value is associated with a key used to retrieve it later.

```swift
// Save the value
UserDefaults.standard.set(true, forKey: "isOnboardingShown")
UserDefaults.standard.set(10, forKey: "tapCount")
UserDefaults.standard.set("Snack", forKey: "userName")

// Read the value
let isOnboardingShown = UserDefaults.standard.bool(forKey: "isOnboardingShown") // true
let tapCount = UserDefaults.standard.integer(forKey: "tapCount") // 10
let userName = UserDefaults.standard.string(forKey: "userName") // "Snack"
```

Stored values remain available after the app closes.

```swift
struct AppStorageStudy: View {

    @State var currentUserName: String?

    var body: some View {
        VStack(spacing: 20) {
            Text(currentUserName ?? "DefaultName")
            Button("Save") {
            	// Data to persist
                let name: String = "Snack"
                currentUserName = name
                // Save the value with a key
                UserDefaults.standard.set(name, forKey: "userName")
            }
        }
        .onAppear {
        	// Read the value for this key
            currentUserName = UserDefaults.standard.string(forKey: "userName")
        }
    }
}
```

## @AppStorage

Declare an `@AppStorage` property with a key to read and write a value through `UserDefaults`.

```swift
struct AppStorageStudy: View {
    // Save the value with a key
    @AppStorage("userName") var currentUserName: String?

    var body: some View {
        VStack(spacing: 20) {
            Text(currentUserName ?? "DefaultName")
            Button("Save") {
                let name: String = "Snack"
                currentUserName = name
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Changes to the property update the stored preference, and SwiftUI updates views that depend on it.

dev-swiftui-ontapgesture.markdown:
A view does not have to look like a button to respond to a tap. `onTapGesture` attaches an action directly to a view, with options for recognizing repeated taps.

## onTapGesture

The supplied closure runs when SwiftUI recognizes the gesture. Unlike a standard button style, attaching a tap gesture does not automatically add a pressed-state highlight.

```swift
struct TapGestureStudy: View {

    @State var isSelected: Bool = false

    var body: some View {
        VStack(spacing: 40){
            RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 32)
                .foregroundColor(isSelected ? .green : .yellow)
                .frame(height: 200)
            // Default button
            Button {
                isSelected.toggle()
            } label: {
                Text("Button")
                    .foregroundColor(.white)
                    .frame(height: 50)
                    .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
                    .background(Color.blue)
                    .cornerRadius(25)
            }
            // Text styled as a button
            Text("Tap Gesture")
                .foregroundColor(.white)
                .frame(height: 50)
                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
                .background(Color.blue)
                .cornerRadius(25)
                // Run when the tap is recognized
                .onTapGesture {
                    isSelected.toggle()
                }
        }
        .padding()
    }
}
```

## Tap count

Set `count` to the number of consecutive taps required before the action runs.

```swift
struct TapGestureStudy: View {

    @State var isSelected: Bool = false

    var body: some View {
        VStack(spacing: 40){
            RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 32)
                .foregroundColor(isSelected ? .green : .yellow)
                .frame(height: 200)
            Text("Tap Gesture")
                .foregroundColor(.white)
                .frame(height: 50)
                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
                .background(Color.blue)
                .cornerRadius(25)
                // Run after three consecutive taps
                .onTapGesture(count: 3) {
                    isSelected.toggle()
                }
        }
        .padding()
    }
}
```

dev-swiftui-if-let-guard-let.markdown:
Both forms unwrap an optional, but they shape the surrounding control flow differently. Choose based on whether the value is needed inside a short conditional branch or throughout the rest of the scope.

## if let

Optional binding runs the branch only when the value is not `nil`. An `else` branch is optional. The unwrapped constant is available inside the successful branch.

```swift
struct UnwrapStudy: View {

    @State var currentUserID: String? = "Snack"
    @State var displayText: String? = nil
    @State var isLoading: Bool = false

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
        	// When displayText is not nil
            if let text = displayText {
                Text(text)
                    .font(.title)
            }
            if isLoading {
                ProgressView()
            }
        }
        .onAppear {
            loadData()
        }
    }

    func loadData() {
		// When currentUserID is not nil
        if let userID = currentUserID {
            isLoading = true
            DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + 3) {
                displayText = "The username is \(userID)."
                isLoading = false
            }
        // When currentUserID is nil
        } else {
            displayText = "There is no user data."
        }
    }
}
```

## guard let

- Use `guard let` inside a scope to check that a required value exists.
- The `else` branch handles failure and must exit the current scope, for example with `return`, `break`, `continue`, or `throw`.
- After the guard succeeds, the unwrapped value is available in the remaining scope.
- Handling failure first keeps the main path less indented and easier to follow.

```swift
struct UnwrapStudy: View {

    @State var currentUserID: String? = "Snack"
    @State var displayText: String? = nil
    @State var isLoading: Bool = false

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
	        // When displayText is not nil
            if let text = displayText {
                Text(text)
                    .font(.title)
            }
            if isLoading {
                ProgressView()
            }
        }
        .onAppear {
            loadData()
        }
    }

    func loadData() {
        guard let userID = currentUserID else {
        	// When currentUserID is nil
            displayText = "There is no user data."
            return // Return from the function
        }
        // When currentUserID is not nil
        isLoading = true
        DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + 3) {
            displayText = "The username is \(userID)."
            isLoading = false
        }
    }
}
```

dev-swfitui-transition.markdown:
Animating a view's position or color is different from animating its insertion or removal. Understanding that distinction also helps explain why a removal transition can sometimes appear not to run.

## Animated transitions

Apply a transition to a conditionally displayed view, then animate the state change that inserts or removes it.

```swift
struct TransitionStudy: View {

    @State var isAnimation: Bool = false

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Button("Button") {
            	// Apply an animation
                withAnimation {
                    isAnimation.toggle()
                }
            }
            Spacer()
            if isAnimation {
                RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 24)
                    .frame(height: UIScreen.main.bounds.height / 2)
                    // Apply a transition
                    .transition(.slide)
            }
        }
        .edgesIgnoringSafeArea(.bottom)
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/c0dfc6279e1b922ce732.gif)

## Transition types

`AnyTransition` provides several effects for inserting and removing views.

```swift
.transition(.opacity)
.transition(.scale)
.transition(.slide)
.transition(.move(edge: .leading))
.transition(.push(from: .leading))
.transition(.offset(y: 100))
```

Use `asymmetric` to give insertion and removal different effects.

```swift
.transition(.asymmetric(
	insertion: .move(edge: .leading), // Insertion transition
	removal: .opacity // Removal transition
))
```

Use `combined` to apply multiple transition effects together.

```swift
.transition(.opacity.combined(with: .scale))
```

## When a removal seems not to animate

In a layered layout, the disappearing view can move behind another view and make its animation look absent. Setting an explicit `zIndex` can preserve the intended stacking order during removal.

```swift
struct TransitionStudy: View {

    @State var isAnimating = false

    var body: some View {
        ZStack {
            Color.green
            	.zIndex(-1) // Set the background's zIndex
                .ignoresSafeArea()
                .onTapGesture {
                    withAnimation(.easeInOut) {
                        isAnimating.toggle()
                    }
                }
            if isAnimating {
                RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 24)
                    .zIndex(1) // Set the foreground's zIndex
                    .transition(.move(edge: .bottom))
                    .onTapGesture {
                        withAnimation(.easeInOut) {
                            isAnimating.toggle()
                        }
                    }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

dev-swiftui-onappear-ondisappear.markdown:
Loading data when a view appears and cleaning up when it leaves are common tasks. These callbacks are simple, but a view can appear more than once, so their timing matters.

## onAppear

Runs the supplied action when the view appears.

```swift
struct OnAppearStudy: View {

    @State var myText: String = "Star Text"

    var body: some View {
        Text(myText)
            .font(.largeTitle)
            // Run when the view appears
            .onAppear(perform: {
            	// Run after three seconds
                DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + 3) {
                    myText = "Next Text"
                }
            })
    }
}
```

In a `LazyVStack`, a child's appearance can trigger its `onAppear` action as that content becomes active. Do not assume this happens only once during the application's lifetime.

```swift
struct OnAppearStudy: View {

    @State var count: Int = 0

    var body: some View {
        NavigationView {
            ScrollView {
            	// Load data on appearance
                LazyVStack {
                    ForEach(0..<20) { _ in
                        RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 20)
                            .frame(height: 200)
                            .padding()
                            // Run on appearance
                            .onAppear {
                                count += 1
                            }
                    }
                }
            }
            .navigationTitle("On Appear \(count)")
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/07648427ed474dc7d67a.png)

## onDisappear

Runs the supplied action when the view disappears.

```swift
struct OnAppearStudy: View {

    @State var myText: String = "Star Text"

    var body: some View {
        Text(myText)
            .font(.largeTitle)
            // Run when the view disappears
            .onDisappear(perform: {
            	myText = "End Text"
            })
    }
}
```

dev-swiftui-actionsheet.markdown:
When a user needs to choose an action, a sheet of options can feel more appropriate than a central alert. The available API depends on the iOS versions the app supports.

## ActionSheet for iOS 13 and 14

`ActionSheet` supports apps targeting iOS 13 and 14. It presents a set of choices, dismisses when an option is selected, and runs the associated action. For newer targets, use `confirmationDialog`.

```swift
struct ActionSheetStudy: View {

    @State var showActionSheet: Bool = false // State property

    var body: some View {
        Button("Click Here!") {
            showActionSheet.toggle() // Set showActionSheet to true
        }
        // ActionSheet view
        .actionSheet(isPresented: $showActionSheet) {
            ActionSheet(
                title: Text("Title"),
                message: Text("This is message."),
                buttons: [
                    .default(Text("Button 1")), // First button
                    .destructive(Text("Button 2")), // Second button
                    .cancel() // Cancel button
                ])
        }
    }
}
```

A `default` button performs an ordinary action. A `cancel` button dismisses without proceeding, while `destructive` marks an action such as deletion that deserves extra attention.

![](/image/development/9eb1b05f95d95a903c6e.png)

## Customizing the options

Extract sheet construction into a function and use an enum to select the appropriate configuration.

```swift
struct ActionSheetStudy: View {

    @State var showActionSheet: Bool = false
    @State var actionSheetType: MyActionSheetType = .isOtherPost

    // Define the action sheet cases
    enum MyActionSheetType {
        case isMyPost
        case isOtherPost
    }

    var body: some View {
        VStack(spacing: 40) {
            Button("My Post") {
                actionSheetType = .isMyPost // MyPost case
                showActionSheet.toggle()
            }
            .actionSheet(isPresented: $showActionSheet, content: getActionSheet)
            Button("Other Post") {
                actionSheetType = .isOtherPost // OtherPost case
                showActionSheet.toggle()
            }
            .actionSheet(isPresented: $showActionSheet, content: getActionSheet)
        }
    }

    func getActionSheet() -> ActionSheet {

        let shareButton: ActionSheet.Button = .default(Text("Share"))
        let reportButton: ActionSheet.Button = .destructive(Text("Report"))
        let deleteButton: ActionSheet.Button = .destructive(Text("Delete"))

        // Build the sheet for the selected case
        switch actionSheetType {
        case .isMyPost:
            return ActionSheet(
                title: Text("My Post"),
                buttons: [
                    shareButton,
                    deleteButton,
                    .cancel()
                ])
        case .isOtherPost:
            return ActionSheet(
                title: Text("Other Post"),
                buttons: [
                    shareButton,
                    reportButton,
                    .cancel()
                ])
        }
    }
}
```

## confirmationDialog for iOS 15 and later

`confirmationDialog` is the newer API for presenting these choices.

```swift
struct ActionSheetStudy: View {

    @State var showActionSheet: Bool = false // State property

    var body: some View {
        Button("Click Here!") {
            showActionSheet.toggle() // Set showActionSheet to true
        }
        // ActionSheet view
        .confirmationDialog("Title", isPresented: $showActionSheet, titleVisibility: .visible) {
            Button("Button 1") {} // First button
            Button("Button 2", role: .destructive) {} // Second button
            Button("Cancel", role: .cancel) {} // Cancel button
        } message: {
            Text("This is message.")
        }
    }
}
```

dev-swiftui-alert.markdown:
An alert may contain only a short message, but its presentation state and button actions can become tangled. Separating simple acknowledgment from a choice between actions keeps the code clearer.

## Acknowledgment alerts

Show a message with a button the user can tap to acknowledge it.

```swift
struct AlertStudy: View {

    @State var showAlert: Bool = false // State property

    var body: some View {
        Button("Click Here!") {
            showAlert.toggle() // Set showAlert to true
        }
        // Alert view
        .alert(isPresented: $showAlert) {
            Alert(title: Text("There was an error!"))
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/1bd309aea5e5f01b58f9.png)

## Alerts with a choice

- Present a message and let the user choose what happens next.
- In the API shown here, `primaryButton` and `secondaryButton` define the two actions.
- `default` is an ordinary button style.
- `cancel` dismisses or abandons the proposed action.
- `destructive` highlights an action such as deletion.

```swift
struct AlertStudy: View {

    @State var showAlert: Bool = false
    @State var backgroundColor: Color = .white

    var body: some View {
        ZStack {
            backgroundColor.edgesIgnoringSafeArea(.all)
            Button("Click Here!") {
                showAlert.toggle()
            }
            // Alert view
            .alert(isPresented: $showAlert) {
                Alert(
                    title: Text("Alert Title"),
                    message: Text("This is message."),
                    // Action button
                    primaryButton: .destructive(Text("Change"), action: {
                        backgroundColor = .yellow
                    }),
                    // Cancel button
                    secondaryButton: .cancel()
                )
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/8800f19c2af2dc8a9430.png)

## Customizing alerts

Extract the alert into a function and supply the title and message appropriate to each action.

```swift
struct AlertStudy: View {

    @State var showAlert: Bool = false
    @State var alertTitle: String = "" // Title property
    @State var alertMessage: String = "" // Message property

    var body: some View {
        VStack(spacing: 40) {
        	// Error button
            Button("Error Button") {
	            // Error Title
                alertTitle = "Error Alert"
                // Error Message
                alertMessage = "This is Error Alert Message."
                showAlert.toggle()
            }
            .alert(isPresented: $showAlert) {
                getAlert()
            }
            // Success button
            Button("Success Button") {
            	// Success Title
                alertTitle = "Success Alert"
                // Success Message
                alertMessage = "This is Success Alert Message."
                showAlert.toggle()
            }
            .alert(isPresented: $showAlert) {
                getAlert()
            }
        }
    }

    // Alert builder
    func getAlert() -> Alert {
        return Alert(
            title: Text(alertTitle),
            message: Text(alertMessage),
            dismissButton: .default(Text("Ok"))
        )
    }
}
```

An enum can represent the different alert configurations.

```swift
struct AlertStudy: View {

    @State var showAlert: Bool = false
    @State var alertType: MyAlert? = nil

    // Define the alert cases
    enum MyAlert {
        case error
        case success
    }

    var body: some View {
        VStack(spacing: 40) {
            Button("Error Button") {
                alertType = .error // Error case
                showAlert.toggle()
            }
            .alert(isPresented: $showAlert) {
                getAlert()
            }
            Button("Success Button") {
                alertType = .success // Success case
                showAlert.toggle()
            }
            .alert(isPresented: $showAlert) {
                getAlert()
            }
        }
    }

    func getAlert() -> Alert {
    	// Build the alert for the selected case
        switch alertType {
        case .error:
            return Alert(
                title: Text("Error Alert"),
                message: Text("This is Error Alert Message."),
                dismissButton: .default(Text("Ok"))
            )
        case .success:
            return Alert(
                title: Text("Success Alert"),
                message: Text("This is Success Alert Message."),
                dismissButton: .default(Text("Ok"))
            )
        default:
            return Alert(title: Text("Error"))
        }
    }
}
```

dev-swiftui-list.markdown:
A list starts as a vertical collection of data, but a real screen often needs groups, deletion, reordering, and new items. Expanding a small shopping list shows where each capability belongs.

## List content

Pass views to a `List` to display them as rows.

```swift
List {
	Text("apple") // First row
	Text("banana") // Second row
	Text("oragne") // Third row
}
```

Use `ForEach` with identifiable data to generate rows dynamically.

```swift
struct ListStudy: View {

    @State var fruits: [String] = [
        "apple", "banana", "orange"
    ]

    var body: some View {
        List {
        	// List displaying the fruits array
            ForEach(fruits, id: \.self) { fruit in
                Text(fruit)
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Sections

A `Section` groups related rows and can include a header and footer.

```swift
struct ListStudy: View {

    @State var fruits: [String] = [
        "apple", "banana", "orange"
    ]

    var body: some View {
        List {
            Section { // Add a section
                ForEach(fruits, id: \.self) { fruit in
                    Text(fruit)
                }
            } header: { // Add a header
                Text("Fruits")
            }
        }
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/299fec0139c2af091fbb.png)

## Deleting rows

`onDelete` receives an `IndexSet` identifying the rows to remove. `IndexSet`, from Foundation, represents a set of integer indices.

```swift
struct ListStudy: View {

    @State var fruits: [String] = [
        "apple", "banana", "orange"
    ]

    var body: some View {
        List {
            Section {
                ForEach(fruits, id: \.self) { fruit in
                    Text(fruit)
                }
                // Delete items from the list
                .onDelete { indexSet in
                    fruits.remove(atOffsets: indexSet)
                }
            } header: {
                Text("Fruits")
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Extract `fruits.remove(atOffsets: indexSet)` into a `delete` function, then pass that function to the `perform` parameter of `onDelete`.

```swift
struct ListStudy: View {

    @State var fruits: [String] = [
        "apple", "banana", "orange"
    ]

    var body: some View {
        List {
            Section {
                ForEach(fruits, id: \.self) { fruit in
                    Text(fruit)
                }
                // Pass delete to the perform parameter
                .onDelete(perform: delete(indexSet:))
            } header: {
                Text("Fruits")
            }
        }
    }

    func delete(indexSet: IndexSet) {
        fruits.remove(atOffsets: indexSet)
    }
}
```

Passing the function directly avoids writing a separate closure just to forward its argument.

```swift
struct ListStudy: View {

    @State var fruits: [String] = [
        "apple", "banana", "orange"
    ]

    var body: some View {
        List {
            Section {
                ForEach(fruits, id: \.self) { fruit in
                    Text(fruit)
                }
                // Pass the delete function directly
                .onDelete(perform: delete)
            } header: {
                Text("Fruits")
            }
        }
    }

    func delete(indexSet: IndexSet) {
        fruits.remove(atOffsets: indexSet)
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/d1a8a1bdbabe33f08920.png)

## Reordering rows

`onMove` lets the user change row order. In this example, the list sits inside a `NavigationView`, and an `EditButton()` enables the editing controls.

```swift
struct ListStudy: View {

    @State var fruits: [String] = [
        "apple", "banana", "orange"
    ]

    var body: some View {
    	// Add a NavigationView
        NavigationView {
            List {
                Section {
                    ForEach(fruits, id: \.self) { fruit in
                        Text(fruit)
                    }
                    .onDelete(perform: delete)
                    // Reorder list items
                    .onMove { indices, newOffset in
                        fruits.move(fromOffsets: indices, toOffset: newOffset)
                    }
                } header: {
                    Text("Fruits")
                }
            }
            .navigationTitle("Grocery List")
            .navigationBarItems(leading: EditButton()) // Add an EditButton
        }
    }

    func delete(indexSet: IndexSet) {
        fruits.remove(atOffsets: indexSet)
    }
}
```

Extract `fruits.move(fromOffsets: indices, toOffset: newOffset)` into a `move` function and pass it to `onMove`, just as with deletion.

```swift
struct ListStudy: View {

    @State var fruits: [String] = [
        "apple", "banana", "orange"
    ]

    var body: some View {
        NavigationView {
            List {
                Section {
                    ForEach(fruits, id: \.self) { fruit in
                        Text(fruit)
                    }
                    .onDelete(perform: delete)
                    // Pass move directly to the perform parameter
                    .onMove(perform: move)
                } header: {
                    Text("Fruits")
                }
            }
            .navigationTitle("Grocery List")
            .navigationBarItems(leading: EditButton())
        }
    }

    func move(indices: IndexSet, newOffset: Int) {
        fruits.move(fromOffsets: indices, toOffset: newOffset)
    }
    func delete(indexSet: IndexSet) {
        fruits.remove(atOffsets: indexSet)
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/7d76eb35615cba531182.png)

## Adding rows

A button in the navigation bar can append an item to the list's data.

```swift
struct ListStudy: View {

    @State var fruits: [String] = [
        "apple", "banana", "orange"
    ]

    var body: some View {
        NavigationView {
            List {
                Section {
                    ForEach(fruits, id: \.self) { fruit in
                        Text(fruit)
                    }
                    .onDelete(perform: delete)
                    .onMove(perform: move)
                } header: {
                    Text("Fruits")
                }
            }
            .navigationTitle("Grocery List")
            .navigationBarItems(
                leading: EditButton(),
                // Add a button for new items
                trailing:
                    Button("Add", action: {
                        add()
                    })
            )
        }
    }

    // Define the add-item function
    func add() {
        fruits.append("peach")
    }
    func move(indices: IndexSet, newOffset: Int) {
        fruits.move(fromOffsets: indices, toOffset: newOffset)
    }
    func delete(indexSet: IndexSet) {
        fruits.remove(atOffsets: indexSet)
    }
}
```

The add button can also be extracted into its own view property.

```swift
struct ListStudy: View {

    @State var fruits: [String] = [
        "apple", "banana", "orange"
    ]

    var body: some View {
        NavigationView {
            List {
                Section {
                    ForEach(fruits, id: \.self) { fruit in
                        Text(fruit)
                    }
                    .onDelete(perform: delete)
                    .onMove(perform: move)
                } header: {
                    Text("Fruits")
                }
            }
            .navigationTitle("Grocery List")
            .navigationBarItems(
                leading: EditButton(),
                trailing: addButton // Add-item button view
            )
        }
    }

    // Define the add-item button view
    var addButton: some View {
        Button("Add") {
            add()
        }
    }

    func add() {
        fruits.append("peach")
    }
    func move(indices: IndexSet, newOffset: Int) {
        fruits.move(fromOffsets: indices, toOffset: newOffset)
    }
    func delete(indexSet: IndexSet) {
        fruits.remove(atOffsets: indexSet)
    }
}
```

![](/image/development/278d30a464d307519f23.png)

## List styles

Apply a `listStyle` modifier to choose the list's overall presentation.

```swift
NavigationView {
	List {
		Section {
			ForEach(fruits, id: \.self) { fruit in
				Text(fruit)
			}
			.onDelete(perform: delete)
			.onMove(perform: move)
		} header: {
			Text("Fruits")
		}
	}
	.listStyle(.grouped) // Change the list style
	.navigationTitle("Grocery List")
	.navigationBarItems(
		leading: EditButton(),
		trailing: addButton
	)
}
```

