dev-swiftui-binding.markdown:
Giving a child a second copy of its parent's state can cause the two values to drift apart. A binding provides access to the original value without creating another storage location.

## @State and @Binding

The parent owns its value with `@State`. The child receives a `@Binding` so it can read and write that same value. Pass the projected binding with the `$` prefix, such as `$isOn`, rather than passing the value alone.

```swift
struct BindingStudy: View { // Parent view

    @State var backgroundColor: Color = Color.green // Declare state

    var body: some View {
        ZStack {
            backgroundColor
                .edgesIgnoringSafeArea(.all)
            ButtonView(backgroundColor: $backgroundColor) // Pass a binding with $

        }
    }
}

struct ButtonView: View { // Child view

    @Binding var backgroundColor: Color // Bind to the state with @Binding

    var body: some View {
        Button {
            backgroundColor = Color.orange // Update the bound value
        } label: {
            Text("Button")
                .foregroundColor(.white)
                .padding()
                .padding(.horizontal)
                .background(.blue)
                .cornerRadius(10)
        }
    }
}
```

dev-swiftui-lazystack.markdown:
Creating every child in a long scrolling layout can spend work on content that is not yet needed. Lazy stacks defer that work and also have different sizing behavior from regular stacks.

## Lazy stacks

A regular stack creates its child views eagerly. `LazyVStack` and `LazyHStack` create children as needed for the layout, rather than constructing them all up front. This is about view creation, not automatic loading of application data.

```swift
ScrollView {
	LazyVStack(alignment: .leading) {
		ForEach(0..<100) { index in
			Text("LazyStack \(index)")
		}
	}
	.background(.blue)
}
```

A lazy stack can occupy the available space along its cross axis, so its layout may differ from a regular stack containing the same views.

![](/image/development/75c1f53924ee6c4178a3.png)

dev-scrollview.markdown:
Once content is larger than its available area, a `ScrollView` lets the user reach the rest. These small examples cover direction and indicator settings.

## Vertical scrolling

The default direction is vertical. Combine `VStack` and `ForEach` to arrange repeated content.

```swift
ScrollView { // Vertical scroll view
	VStack {
		ForEach(0..<20) { index in
			Rectangle()
				.fill(Color.blue)
				.frame(height: 200)
		}
	}
}
```

## Horizontal scrolling

Set the `axes` parameter to `.horizontal` to scroll sideways. Use `showsIndicators` to control whether the scroll indicator is visible.

```swift
// Horizontal scrolling without indicators
ScrollView(.horizontal, showsIndicators: false, content: {
	HStack {
		ForEach(0..<20) { index in
			Rectangle()
				.fill(Color.blue)
				.frame(width: 300)
		}
	}
})
```

dev-swiftui-foreach.markdown:
Instead of writing the same view repeatedly, define the relationship between data and presentation once. `ForEach` can work with a simple range or with identifiable elements in a collection.

## Repeating a view

Provide a range and a view builder to create content for each value.

```swift
VStack {
	ForEach(0..<10) { index in // Set the iteration range
		Text("Hi, my index is \(index)") // Repeated view
	}
}
```

## Using an array

An array supplies both the values and the number of rows. Its `.indices` property provides valid indices. `id: \.self` uses each supplied value as its identity, so those values must be unique and stable for the intended updates.

```swift
struct ForEachStudy: View {

    let data: [String] = ["Hi", "Hello", "Hey"]

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            ForEach(data.indices, id: \.self) { index in // Iterate over the array
                Text("\(index) is \(data[index])") // Use the array value for each index
            }
        }
    }
}
```

dev-swiftui-init-enum.markdown:
Cards that differ only in color or wording do not need separate view types. Initializers expose those differences, while enums limit configuration to meaningful choices.

## Default property values

Properties such as `title` and `backgroundColor` make a view reusable. Assign defaults when a standard configuration is useful.

```swift
struct TestView: View {

    // Assign default property values
    let backgroundColor: Color = .red
    let title: String = "Apple"

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text(title)
                .font(.largeTitle)
                .foregroundColor(.white)
        }
        .frame(width: 150, height: 150)
        .background(backgroundColor)
        .cornerRadius(30)
    }
}
struct TestView_Previews: PreviewProvider {
    static var previews: some View {
        TestView() // Create an instance
    }
}
```

## Initializers

An initializer supplies the values an instance needs. `self` refers to the current instance, so `self.backgroundColor` is its stored property. Swift structures can receive a synthesized memberwise initializer when a custom one is not provided.

```swift
struct TestView: View {

    let backgroundColor: Color
    let title: String

    // Initializer
    init(backgroundColor: Color, title: String) {
        self.backgroundColor = backgroundColor
        self.title = title
    }

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text(title)
                .font(.largeTitle)
                .foregroundColor(.white)
        }
        .frame(width: 150, height: 150)
        .background(backgroundColor)
        .cornerRadius(30)
    }
}

struct TestView_Previews: PreviewProvider {
    static var previews: some View {
        TestView(backgroundColor: .red, title: "Apple") // Supply initial values
    }
}
```

Related properties can be configured together during initialization.

```swift
struct TestView: View {

    let backgroundColor: Color
    let title: String

    init(title: String) {
        self.title = title
        // Initialize backgroundColor from the title
        if title == "Apple" {
            self.backgroundColor = .red
        } else {
            self.backgroundColor = .orange
        }
    }

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text(title)
                .font(.largeTitle)
                .foregroundColor(.white)
        }
        .frame(width: 150, height: 150)
        .background(backgroundColor)
        .cornerRadius(30)
    }
}

struct TestView_Previews: PreviewProvider {
    static var previews: some View {
        TestView(title: "Orage") // Supply initial values
    }
}
```

## Enumerations

An enum defines a limited set of meaningful configurations. Use its cases to select related content and appearance values together.

```swift
struct TestView: View {

    let backgroundColor: Color
    let title: String

    // Define the available configurations as an enum
    enum Fruit {
        case apple
        case orange
        case grape
    }

    init(fruit: Fruit) { // Initialize with an enum case
	    // Initialize the properties from the supplied configuration
        if fruit == .apple {
            self.title = "Apple"
            self.backgroundColor = .red
        } else if fruit == .orange {
            self.title = "Orange"
            self.backgroundColor = .orange
        } else {
            self.title = "Grape"
            self.backgroundColor = .purple
        }
    }

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text(title)
                .font(.largeTitle)
                .foregroundColor(.white)
        }
        .frame(width: 150, height: 150)
        .background(backgroundColor)
        .cornerRadius(30)
    }
}

struct TestView_Previews: PreviewProvider {
    static var previews: some View {
    	VStack {
        	TestView(fruit: .grape) // Initialize the grape configuration
			TestView(fruit: .apple) // Initialize the apple configuration
    	}
    }
}
```

dev-20221031.markdown:
Conditionals let a program choose behavior based on its current state. Swift uses `if-else` for Boolean conditions and `switch` for matching a value against patterns.

### Control flow with if-else

An `if` condition must be a `Bool`; parentheses are optional. Conditions are checked in order, and only the first matching branch runs.

~~~swift
let score = 75

if score >= 80 {
    print("A")
} else if score >= 60 {
    print("B")
} else {
    print("C")
}
~~~

### Pattern matching with switch

A `switch` is useful when comparing one value against several values or ranges. It must be exhaustive, so add `default` if the listed cases do not cover every possibility.

~~~swift
switch score {
case 80...100: print("A")
case 60..<80: print("B")
default: print("Keep going")
}
~~~

Each case ends automatically. Use `fallthrough` only when execution should deliberately continue into the next case. Use `if-else` for varied conditions and `switch` when matching a single value against several patterns.

dev-20221026-pojlxcb6.markdown:
Loops repeat work. The right form depends on whether there is a collection to traverse and whether the condition should be checked before or after each iteration.

### Traversing collections with for-in

~~~swift
let numbers = [2, 4, 6]
for number in numbers {
    print(number)
}

let words = ["elephant": "코끼리", "lion": "사자"]
for (english, korean) in words {
    print("\(english): \(korean)")
}
~~~

A dictionary element is a key–value tuple. Do not rely on a particular dictionary iteration order.

### Conditional loops with while

~~~swift
var count = 0
while count < 3 {
    count += 1
}

repeat {
    count -= 1
} while count > 0
~~~

`while` checks its condition first, so the body may never run. `repeat-while` checks afterward, so the body runs at least once. Use `for-in` for a sequence of values and a while loop when a condition controls repetition.
